```java
// Anti-pattern: every iteration is a separate network round-trip + commit
con.setAutoCommit(true);                       // each statement commits on its own
try (PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement(sql)) {
    for (User u : users) {                     // 50,000 users
        ps.setString(1, u.name());
        ps.setString(2, u.email());
        ps.executeUpdate();                    // 50,000 round-trips, 50,000 commits
    }
}
```

```java
// Right: queue rows with addBatch, send them together, commit once
con.setAutoCommit(false);
try (PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement(sql)) {
    int n = 0;
    for (User u : users) {
        ps.setString(1, u.name());
        ps.setString(2, u.email());
        ps.addBatch();                         // queued, no round-trip yet
        if (++n % 1000 == 0) ps.executeBatch(); // flush every 1000
    }
    ps.executeBatch();                         // remaining rows
    con.commit();                              // one atomic commit
}
```

*Same rows, but round-trips drop from ~50,000 to ~50 and you commit once instead of per row.*

```java
String sql = "INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES (?, ?)";
con.setAutoCommit(false);
try (PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement(sql)) {
    int n = 0;
    for (User u : users) {
        ps.setString(1, u.name());
        ps.setString(2, u.email());
        ps.addBatch();                       // queue
        if (++n % 1000 == 0) ps.executeBatch(); // flush every 1000
    }
    ps.executeBatch();                       // remaining
    con.commit();
} catch (SQLException e) {
    con.rollback();
    throw e;
} finally {
    con.setAutoCommit(true);
}
```

